    "guide": "AI Product and Program Management is the job of turning machine learning capability into a shipped feature or product, with a roadmap, deadlines, and a plan for how engineering, data science, and design coordinate to get there. On a given week the work means writing specs for a model backed feature, sequencing what the ML team builds against what the platform team needs to serve it, and running the reviews that decide whether a model is ready to launch. This is different from Applied AI and Solutions Engineering, which sits closer to the technical build itself, integrating models into customer workflows or writing the code that connects a model to a product. It is also different from AI Company Operations, which runs the internal machinery of an AI company, finance, hiring, vendor and compute management, rather than owning what customers see. A Product and Program Manager sits between those two worlds, translating what the model can actually do into what the business will ship and by when.\n\nOur current sample of postings does not show CSPO, PMP, or any other product management certification named as a requirement, so we will not claim one is standard here. Several postings do ask for a technical or computer science background, especially titles carrying Technical Product Manager or Technical Program Manager, which appear repeatedly from companies like Bjak, Cohere, and ServiceNow. Beyond that, the data we pulled does not specify degree requirements in a way we can quote reliably. What we can say honestly is that the credential most postings actually lean on is a track record of shipping AI or ML adjacent products, not a particular diploma or badge.\n\nThe postings in our data skew senior. Titles cluster around Senior, Staff, Principal, and Sr Staff Product Manager, alongside plain Manager and Lead tags, with comparatively few roles labeled for someone starting out. That pattern suggests companies are hiring AI Product and Program Managers who already have general product or program management experience and are adding AI specific scope on top of it, rather than training someone into the discipline from zero. A smaller set of postings use Product Owner or Mid level tags, mostly in Europe, which is where an earlier career path into this work looks most open right now.\n\nThe skills tags attached to these postings point to Large Language Models most often, followed by AWS, Python, SQL, and Model Evaluation, with Retrieval Augmented Generation, Prompt Engineering, Fine tuning, and MLOps showing up regularly as well. Governance and safety terms appear too, including AI Governance, NIST, and Red Teaming, particularly at companies building AI for regulated or public sector customers, such as Cohere's public sector and defense focused roles. Platform specific skills like Databricks, Hugging Face, PyTorch, and LangChain show up tied to the companies that build on those stacks. The throughline is that a Product and Program Manager in this discipline needs enough technical fluency to evaluate a model's output and understand its limits, even without writing the production code themselves.\n\nAs of today we currently track approximately 43 open postings in this category. Only 3 of the 159 postings we reviewed carried an actual salary figure, all three from Adzuna sourced US listings, ranging from $91,251 to $158,072 a year. That is too thin a slice to call a market rate, so we are naming it honestly rather than dressing it up as a trend. Bjak, ServiceNow, Scale AI, Databricks, and Cohere were the companies posting most often for this discipline in our sample, with Bjak alone accounting for a large share through repeated postings across its UK and Germany offices for the same handful of AI finance and investing app roles.",
